<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>uk.mathewcsims.podman-autostart</string>
    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/bin/sh</string>
        <string>/Users/mathewcsims/self-hosted/autostart/podman-autostart.sh</string>
    </array>
    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- LOAD-BEARING. Without this the VM this job exists to start can be
         killed moments after it starts, by launchd, for doing its job.

         From launchd.plist(5): "When a job dies, launchd kills any remaining
         processes with the same process group ID as the job. Setting this key
         to true disables that behavior."

         `podman machine start` spawns gvproxy and krunkit and returns. Those
         two ARE the VM, they outlive the command, and they do NOT setsid away
         — confirmed live: both run with PPID 1 (podman itself exited and they
         were reparented) but keep the PGID of the podman invocation that
         started them. Inside a launchd job that group is the job's group, so
         when this script finishes its container-status listing and exits,
         launchd tears the group down and takes the VM with it.

         Observed 2026-08-08: script exited 10:56:12 having reported all 31
         containers up; podman-watchdog found the VM dead at 10:57:41. Every
         Mac-hosted app was down. No crash report for either process, because
         they were signalled rather than crashing. Re-running the SAME script
         by hand from an interactive shell worked and stayed up — the process
         group belonged to a shell session, so there was no launchd job for it
         to be reaped against. Same script, same command, opposite outcome:
         that difference is the whole diagnosis.

         Not deterministic — the stack has survived plenty of reboots, so
         there is a race between launchd's teardown and podman's children
         being reparented. This removes the race rather than winning it.
         podman-watchdog/ remains the backstop for VM deaths from other
         causes; this prevents the one we can name. -->
    <key>AbandonProcessGroup</key>
    <true/>

    <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
    <dict>
        <key>HOME</key>
        <string>/Users/mathewcsims</string>
        <key>PATH</key>
        <string>/opt/podman/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin</string>
    </dict>
    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>/Users/mathewcsims/self-hosted/autostart/launchd.log</string>
    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>/Users/mathewcsims/self-hosted/autostart/launchd.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
